The API provides fields related to:

job title and normalized title;
company and organization;
posting date;
city, state, and geographic identifiers;
remote status;
employment type;
salary;
SOC and O*NET occupation classifications;
NAICS industry classification;
skills;
job description;
education and work-related signals.

The cleaned dataset retains the fields most directly related to the project’s market-baseline and skill-gap analyses.

The project generates and uses the following analytical outputs:

data/processed/data_scientist_software_publishers.csv — cleaned output from the initial API pipeline;
data/processed/data_dictionary.csv — variable descriptions from the initial pipeline;
data/processed/skill_summary.csv — initial technical-skill summary;
data/module3/data_scientist_software_publishers_clean.csv — enhanced 1,016-posting analytical dataset;
data/module3/top20_market_skills.csv — most frequently requested employer skills; and
data/module3/team_skill_market_demand.csv — employer demand for the technical skills used in the team comparison.

The large raw Module 3 dataset is kept out of version control. Analysis pages use the smaller filtered and processed outputs to reduce rendering time and avoid GitHub Pages issues.

Machine learning methods can extend the descriptive job-market analysis by identifying patterns among postings and helping organize opportunities into meaningful groups.
At the current stage of the project, the primary focus is exploratory analysis and skill-gap assessment. The cleaned dataset and variables developed in Modules 2 and 3 establish the foundation for more advanced modeling in later project phases.



Future analysis may include:

clustering job postings based on skills, experience, salary, and other characteristics;
grouping employers or roles with similar hiring profiles;
identifying segments of opportunities that may be more attainable for early-career candidates;
developing a structured career-fit or opportunity score; and
comparing job-market segments based on compensation and skill requirements.




Potential variables for modeling include:

salary midpoint;
minimum years of experience;
education requirements;
remote-work classification;
employer;
geographic location; and
technical skill indicators.

These features can help distinguish different types of opportunities within the Data Scientist occupational market in Software Publishers.



